Q: Flutter image_picker PlatformException(missing_valid_image_uri, Cannot find at least one of the selected images., null, null) A: <p><strong>This error occurs because the image_picker can't receive the image URI back from the native Activity.</strong></p>
<p>1. Check <code>launchMode</code> in <code>AndroidManifest.xml</code></p>
<p>If your app's <code>launchMode</code> is set to <code>singleInstance</code>, the image_picker won't be able to return the result. Change it to:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;activity
    android:name=&quot;.MainActivity&quot;
    android:launchMode=&quot;singleTask&quot;&gt; &lt;!-- or singleTop --&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>2. Implement <code>retrieveLostData()</code></p>
<p>If the OS kills your Activity while picking images, you lose the data. Add this to recover it:</p>
<pre><code>Future&lt;void&gt; getLostData() async {
  final ImagePicker picker = ImagePicker();
  final LostDataResponse response = await picker.retrieveLostData();
  if (response.isEmpty) return;

  final List&lt;XFile&gt;? files = response.files;
  if (files != null) {
    adImages.addAll(files.map((e) =&gt; e.path));
    emit(CreateAdChangedImagesState());
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Call this in your <code>initState</code> or when the app launches.</p>
<p>3. Update <code>image_picker</code> to the latest version</p>
<p>4. Don't use <code>limit: 1</code> with <code>pickMultiImage()</code></p>
<p>If you're using <code>pickMultiImage(limit: 1)</code>, this method will throw an exception. For single image picking, use <code>pickImage()</code> instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>final XFile? image = await picker.pickImage(source: ImageSource.gallery);
</code></pre>

Q: How to foreach a hasMany relationship in Laravel 6 A: <p>My problem was exactly what you got. I can't loop collection from hasMany relationship.</p>
<p>I'd solved by converting data into object instead of collection.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function convertToObject($data) {
    return json_decode(json_encode($data), FALSE);
}
public function object() {
    $objek = \App\Objek::all();
    $objek = $this-&gt;convertToObject($objek);

    return view('pages.alat.object.object')-&gt;with('data', $objek);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Having issue with my state not appearing on my first render until I refresh A: <p><code>useState()</code> only uses its initializer on the first render.</p>
<p>Most likely, during client-side navigation <code>product</code> is initially missing/old and arrives later, so this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const [imageChanges, setImageChanges] = useState(
    mode === &quot;update&quot; &amp;&amp; product
        ? product.product_images.map(...)
        : []
)
</code></pre>
<p>initializes to <code>[]</code> and is never updated. On refresh, <code>product</code> is already available on the first render, which explains why the images appear.</p>
<p>Sync the image state when the product changes:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>useEffect(() =&gt; {
    if (mode === &quot;update&quot; &amp;&amp; product) {
        setImageChanges(
            product.product_images.map(img =&gt; ({
                type: &quot;db&quot;,
                image: { ...img, isDeleted: false },
            }))
        )
    }
}, [mode, product])
</code></pre>
<p>Alternatively, if each product form should have completely fresh state, key it by product id:</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;GlobalProductForm
    key={product.id}
    product={product}
    ...
/&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Also remove this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>useLayoutEffect(() =&gt; {
    return () =&gt; setImageChanges([])
}, [])
</code></pre>
<p>Setting state while the component is unmounting is unnecessary and can cause its own problems.</p>
